e future of europe and  that is a message he will be driving  home to the americans today rebecca of  course securing those weapons is one  thing uh but one imagines physically  distributing them to the areas where  they're most needed will be  extraordinarily challenging  absolutely as you can imagine big  weapons and also trying to do it under  the cover of darkness this war you know  is one of course where nobody wants the  the the distribution the logistics to  become public you know ukraine does not  want russia to know just how and where  these weapons are going so we're getting  minor bits of information about what's  trickling into the country but in terms  of distribution lines or logistics  routes we just don't have the answers to  those questions you know they're being  transported by rail and road did see  some tanks the other day on a train rail  um you know going through the country  but they're going through civilian areas  michael so obviously a target and nobody  wants the russians to know where they're  going  rebecca stay with us more questions for  you uh in a moment but first we'd like  to give our viewers a look at your  report on ukraine as the nation marks  orthodox easter day you visited one  village northeast of kiev that was under  russian control until a few weeks ago  that community came together to build a  makeshift chapel after russian soldiers  destroyed their church  [Music]  recall and reflection for jesus who  sacrificed all to die on the cross  it's the saddest day in the christian  calendar for the worshipers here in this  village outside kiev their good friday  service in this makeshift chapel has  taken on an even deeper meaning this  year  [Music]  russian soldiers marching on kiev burnt  their 19th century church to the ground  the missile hit the altar of the church  on the eastern side of the building  the damage spread inside the windows  exploded out  it was a direct hit  when the church was burning i thought my  soul was burning  the icon of the prophet elijah the only  thing to survive  it survived under the ruins  under the ashes completely  ukraine is a deeply religious country in  villages like this one the church plays  a central role in the community  these people tell me that while russians  may have destroyed their church they  will never destroy their spirit  the church is our soul we grew up here  when the church was burning right before  our eyes we were very scared  the scariest thing of all was that we  couldn't do anything because we were  under fire  all my childhood was spent here i grew  up three houses down the road so how  should i feel  it's like something is gone from your  life  they vow they'll rebuild their beloved  church in all its former glory once  they're certain the russians won't  return  if they do the priest has a message  straight from the bible  jesus said love one another as i have  loved you  but he also said something else  he who fights by the sword shall die by  the sword  as the service ends they pray for christ  reborn on easter sunday a church rebuilt  and doubtless for a homeland free once  again  [Music]  rebecca today as ukraine endures now the  third month of the russian invasion how  are people spending this rather  extraordinary orthodox easter  well it certainly is an easter like no  other michael we've been here i'm  standing on the forecourt in fact of  michael's church it's one of the biggest  uh cathedrals here in kiev there have  been a lot of people here today  streaming in and out we've been speaking  to some of them asking them whether  they're afraid this year because of the  heightened security the heightened  threats of a potential attack many  people saying that they are concerned  understandably of course but that  they're happy to be able to come out and  celebrate their faith as they do every  year uh we have noticed of course that  the service is different this year  normally there is a midnight mass the  night befo